<Base title="Fonseca Laboratory  @  UVic" flush>
	<img class="banner" src="/images/dp/cadboro_banner.jpg" alt="Fonseca Lab banner" />
	<div class="prose">
	<p class="lead">
		The Fonseca Lab studies how cancer-driving DNA alterations shape the immune microenvironment of
		prostate cancer. We use genomic profiling and spatial imaging technologies to probe how cancer cells
		with specific genetic alterations communicate with and modulate immune cells in the tumour
		microenvironment. Our projects are anchored in human prostate cancer biology, leveraging unique
		biobanked and clinically defined patient cohorts to ensure clinical relevance. Our goal is to build a
		combined genomic and immune biomarker framework that will refine how immunotherapies are developed and selected
		for patients with metastatic prostate cancer.
		<br><br>
		We also study a rare inherited form of kidney cancer caused by inactivation of the tumour suppressor
		gene von Hippel-Lindau (VHL). Our lab hosts the biospecimen repository for the BC Cancer von Hippel
		Lindau Biobank which drives research into this rare disease. Our projects delve into the spatial
		organisation of the VHL-associated immune microenvironment and the immune correlates of response to
		belzutifan treatment.
		<br><br>
		The lab is located at the Deeley Research Centre (part of BC Cancer-Victoria) on beautiful Vancouver
		Island. Our translational research benefits from close ties with colleagues at the University of
		Victoria, the Vancouver Prostate Centre, Vancouver General Hospital and BC Cancer-Vancouver.
	</p>

	<p class="acknowledgement">
		BC Cancer-Victoria and the University of Victoria are located on the traditional and unceded territory
		of the Lək̓ʷəŋən Peoples, known today as the Esquimalt and Songhees Nations.
	</p>
	</div>
</Base>
